<!--
Beetmania, a fan site for beets declared entirely in markup: recipes, growing
guides, a beet counter and a blog. Deployed at https://beetmania.beet.org, its
blog published as a standard.site publication on the `beetmania.beet.org`
atproto account.

The root is a one-shot `<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>` dispatcher whose
`Router` is its child, so the entry is a set of commands and the served site is
ONE of them: `serve`. `always=true` dispatches on every boot whatever `--server`
says, since `--server` selects the served site's transports, not this entry's.
A `CliServer` refuses a flag the command does not take, naming the nearest
one it does, rather than running without it.

    beet --entry=examples/beetmania serve --server=http   # the site over http
    beet --entry=examples/beetmania serve --server=tui    # ..as a live terminal
    beet --entry=examples/beetmania --help                # every command

## The served site

`<Route path="serve" {(HttpServer, TuiServer, SshTuiServer, DomServer)}>` is the
serve route and the server host in one. Its `Router` child is the site, a url
space of its own rooted at `/`, so no http request can reach a sibling command:
the publish verbs below are commands of this entry, never routes of the site.
The router's middleware attaches as component spreads (request logging, the
cache policy, `--help`, terminal link navigation, the layout), `<RoutesDir/>`
discovers the pages in
`routes/`, and the blog is a subtree wearing `templates/ArticleLayout.bsx` inside
the site `Layout`.

`<ServeBlobs prefix="repo"/>` publishes this directory read-only at `/repo`, the
store the browser process reads this entry through, and `<AssetsDir src="wasm">`
mounts the wasm binary the page boots: `wasm/beet-ui.{wasm,js}` link to the build
output in `target/wasm`, which `just build-wasm-ui` provides and a deploy builds
before publishing (`<WasmRuntime/>`). The page's `<Wasm route="serve"/>` boots
this same entry in the tab, dispatching `serve` with the page's own url as the
opening route (see `templates/Layout.bsx`).

## The assets

`assets/logo/logo.svg`, the beet, is the one asset this directory owns.
`<IconSet/>` derives every icon size from it (the harvest post's cover is its
largest), `<SocialCards/>` draws a link preview per page in a font borrowed from
the workspace's `assets/`, and `beet check` fails a derived file that is
missing. The `assets` verb borrows and derives them into this directory for a
local serve; the deploy does the same into the copy it publishes, so nothing it
writes is committed.

    just beetmania assets   # borrow the font, derive the icons and cards

## The deploy

The site runs on AWS Lambda behind Cloudflare at `beetmania.beet.org`, the
`beetmania` stack. A `<LambdaSiteBlock>` publishes its `authorities` only from
a production stage, so the example deploys as `--stage=prod` of its own app; any
other stage answers only at its gateway url. The deploy machinery sits behind
`bx:cfg`, absent from a lean binary that only serves.

Cloudflare's edge caches what the origin's `Cache-Control` allows:
`CacheHeadersMiddleware` + `CacheHeaders` mark html edge-cacheable for a week,
and `cache="1h"` the assets and the wasm. So everything that changes what is
served purges this stack's hostnames from the edge (`<CloudflarePurgeCache/>`,
never the site's, which shares the zone): the deploy after its apply,
and `sync` after `<LambdaRestart/>`, which retires the function's warm
environments so the next request boots afresh, re-discovering the routes and
re-reading this document and the publication's index, all of which a boot
resolves once.

    just beetmania deploy --stage=prod   # bucket, site, function, hostname
    just beetmania deploy --stage=prod --elevated   # ..converging this entry's deploy credentials first
    just beetmania sync --stage=prod     # republish the site, no redeploy
    just beetmania watch --stage=prod    # tail the function's logs
    just beetmania destroy --stage=prod --force

## The publication

The blog is a standard.site publication on `beetmania.beet.org`'s repo, one
document per dated post, each document's `content` the post as a beet scene
(`org.beet.scene#body`). `beetlover.beet.org` is a contributor who reposts each
announcement it is credited on and replies to it, so the comments island under
each post has something to show. The README's "Test accounts" lists both.
`social/publish` converges the repo
to the posts it names and rewrites the committed index the served site reads
(`derived/standard-site.json`), which lands with the next `sync`.

    just beetmania social deploy --stage=prod                                  # the handle records
    just beetmania social publish --only=<slug> --announce=<slug> --dry-run   # read every line first
    just beetmania social publish --only=<slug> --announce=<slug>             # publish and announce
    just beetmania social probe --stage=prod                                   # the handle, then the live site

The publisher's app password is `BEETMANIA_BEET_ORG_APP_PASSWORD` and the
contributor's `BEETLOVER_BEET_ORG_APP_PASSWORD`, both `env_var` records in the
`agents` group of this entry's own `secrets.toml`, loaded by the top-level
`<Secrets/>` below. The repo's `secrets.toml` keeps a copy of each for the live
atproto tests, which write to the same test org.

## The secrets

This entry owns its `secrets.toml`, beside this file: a secrets document
holding a deploy credential belongs to exactly one entry, so the example's
credentials are its own and reach none of the site's resources. It holds the
two app passwords, `TF_STATE_PASSPHRASE` for both stacks' state, and the deploy
credentials an elevated deploy seals (`beetmania-deploy` at AWS and at
Cloudflare). The `secrets` verbs address it:

    just beetmania secrets/check   # does this machine's identity open it
    just beetmania secrets/ls      # what is there, and how each rolls

## The zone audit

The example's records live in the `beet.org` zone beside the site's, so its
audit answers for the names it owns and nothing else: a record under them that
no stack here declares is its stray, and the site's records are never its to
judge or delete.

    just beetmania audit --stage=prod   # the zone holds exactly what we declare, under our names
-->
<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>
<Router {HelpHandler}>
	<!-- this entry's own secrets document, loaded into the process
	     environment before this entry builds; a machine without the identity
	     loads nothing -->
	<Secrets/>
	<Route path="secrets" bx:cfg="feature:vault"><SecretsRoutes/></Route>
	<!-- the app identity: the deploy credentials are named for it
	     (`beetmania-deploy`), the stacks below name their own apps -->
	<PackageConfig
		title="Beetmania"
		app_name="beetmania"
		description="Recipes, growing guides and gentle warnings for people who love beets"
		homepage="https://beetmania.beet.org"/>
	<!-- every icon size derived from the one source image -->
	<IconSet src="assets/logo/logo.svg"/>
	<!-- the tags a post may carry; `beet check` fails any other -->
	<DocMetaRules tags={["recipes", "growing", "news"]}/>
	<!-- the publisher, a test org, and the contributor, a test user who
	     reposts and replies: each handle, did and display name written
	     literally (see the README's "Test accounts") -->
	<AtprotoAccount bx:ref="beetmania" handle="beetmania.beet.org"
		did="did:plc:7bxw2ljdoqcny4rio7oiqsru" display_name="Beetmania"
		secret="BEETMANIA_BEET_ORG_APP_PASSWORD"/>
	<AtprotoAccount bx:ref="beetlover" handle="beetlover.beet.org"
		did="did:plc:xfhml5eyathkdgloyiresv4d" display_name="Beet Lover"
		secret="BEETLOVER_BEET_ORG_APP_PASSWORD" auto_repost=true/>
	<Route path="serve" {(HttpServer, TuiServer, SshTuiServer, DomServer)}>
		<Router {(RequestLogger, CacheHeadersMiddleware, CacheHeaders, HelpHandler, NavigateHandler, Layout(Document("templates/Layout.bsx")))}>
			<Theme color={Srgba(Srgba{red:0.61, green:0.12, blue:0.29, alpha:1.0})}/>
			<Instance document="templates/Styles.bsx"/>
			<DefaultAppRoutes/>
			<Sitemap/>
			<ServerIslands/>
			<!-- the blog as a standard.site publication; its served half reads
			     the index `social/publish` writes -->
			<StandardSitePub bx:ref="blog" {AccountRef($beetmania)} path="blog"
				name="The Beetmania Almanac" description="News, harvests and the odd experiment from the Beetmania patch."/>
			<SocialCards font="assets/fonts/FiraSans-Bold.ttf"/>
			<RoutesDir src="routes" filter={GlobFilter{exclude:["blog/**"]}}/>
			<Route path="blog" {Layout(Document("templates/ArticleLayout.bsx"))}>
				<RoutesDir src="routes/blog"/>
			</Route>
			<ServeBlobs prefix="repo"/>
			<AssetsDir src="assets" cache="1h"/>
			<AssetsDir src="wasm" prefix="wasm" cache="1h"/>
		</Router>
	</Route>
	<!-- the font the assets borrow from the workspace and the files they derive, into this
	     directory for a local serve -->
	<Route path="assets" bx:cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra" {ExchangeSequence}>
		<DirCopy src="assets" dest="examples/beetmania/assets" paths="fonts/FiraSans-Bold.ttf"/>
		<Derive/>
	</Route>
	<!-- the deploy machinery, absent from a binary that only serves, carrying
	     the provider addresses its stacks deploy into, resolved by ancestry:
	     the region, the Cloudflare account and the `beet.org` zone the
	     site's hostname and handle records live in. On the fragment rather
	     than the root, so the browser runtime, which links none of them, never
	     meets them. -->
	<Fragment bx:cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra" {(AwsRegion("us-west-2"), CloudflareAccount("74aba4da669f57fc6fc3e63ebcfcff26"), CloudflareZone{domain:"beet.org", id:"45905c861559d9eb8c9b72ee87adca7b"})}>
	<!-- the repo's AWS account, shared with `site/`, and this entry's own
	     deploy credentials, `beetmania-deploy` at each provider, sealed in
	     this entry's document and lowered from the stacks below alone -->
	<AwsAccount name="beet"/>
	<AwsDeployCredential/>
	<CloudflareDeployCredential/>
	<!-- the deploy: a lambda serving the repo bucket at `beetmania.beet.org` -->
	<Stack app_name="beetmania">
		<!-- the block + its build artifact share one entity, booting `serve` -->
		<LambdaSiteBlock features="lambda,atproto" authorities="beetmania.beet.org" exec_route="serve"/>
		<S3BucketBlock label="repo" {RepoStoreBlock}/>
		<Group bx:ref="beetmania_up">
			<TofuApply layer="storage"/>
			<WasmRuntime package="beet-cli" bin="beet" features="web_ui" out="target/wasm/beet-ui.wasm"/>
			<RepoAssemble src="examples/beetmania">
				<DirCopy src="assets" dest="assets" paths="fonts/FiraSans-Bold.ttf"/>
				<Derive/>
			</RepoAssemble>
			<RepoSync/>
			<TofuApply/>
			<!-- after the apply rolls the function, so the edge refills from it -->
			<CloudflarePurgeCache/>
			<LambdaWatch timeout="30s"/>
		</Group>
		<Group bx:ref="beetmania_down">
			<StackTeardown/>
			<TofuDestroy/>
		</Group>
		<DeployRoutes deploy={$beetmania_up} destroy={$beetmania_down}/>
		<!-- a content-only republish into the version the running function reads -->
		<Route path="sync" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<AdoptCurrentDeploy/>
			<WasmRuntime package="beet-cli" bin="beet" features="web_ui" out="target/wasm/beet-ui.wasm"/>
			<RepoAssemble src="examples/beetmania">
				<DirCopy src="assets" dest="assets" paths="fonts/FiraSans-Bold.ttf"/>
				<Derive/>
			</RepoAssemble>
			<RepoSync/>
			<!-- a page's bytes are read per request, so the sync above is live
			     already; the restart is for what a boot resolves once: route
			     discovery, this document, the publication's index. Before the
			     purge, since purging while the old environments serve
			     re-caches them. -->
			<LambdaRestart/>
			<CloudflarePurgeCache/>
		</Route>
		<Route path="watch" {ExchangeSequence}>
			<LambdaWatch/>
		</Route>
	</Stack>
	<!-- the handles and the publication: their own stack, since the handle
	     records share nothing with the function but the zone, and only
	     `--stage=prod` publishes them -->
	<Route path="social" bx:cfg="feature:atproto">
		<Stack app_name="beetmania-social">
			<Group bx:ref="social_up">
				<TofuApply/>
			</Group>
			<Group bx:ref="social_down">
				<StackTeardown/>
				<TofuDestroy/>
			</Group>
			<DeployRoutes deploy={$social_up} destroy={$social_down}/>
			<AtprotoHandleBlock domain="beet.org" dns_stage="prod" accounts={[$beetmania, $beetlover]}/>
			<Route path="publish" {(ExchangeSequence, PublicationRef($blog))}>
				<StandardSitePublish/>
				<StandardSiteAnnounce/>
				<StandardSiteIndexWrite/>
			</Route>
			<Route path="probe" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<AtprotoHandleProbe/>
				<StandardSiteProbe {PublicationRef($blog)}/>
			</Route>
		</Stack>
	</Route>
	<!-- the audit of the names this entry owns in the `beet.org` zone: its
	     hostname, everything beneath it (the handle record, the certificate's
	     validation records) and its contributor's handle record -->
	<Route path="audit" {ExchangeSequence}>
		<ZoneAudit scope="Zone" names={["beetmania.beet.org", "*.beetmania.beet.org", "_atproto.beetlover.beet.org"]}/>
	</Route>
	</Fragment>
</Router>
</CliServer>
